---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { listTools, listTags, listVerticals } from "../lib/content-api";

const tools = await listTools();
const tags = await listTags();
const verticals = await listVerticals();
const pages = 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + tools.length + tags.length + verticals.length;
---

<Base
  title="How this was built"
  description="Variant A of the Lossless Toolkit rebuild: every view prerendered, zero client-side JavaScript, and the client-name suppression enforced at the ingest boundary rather than in templates."
  path="/about/"
  image="/og/fallback-6.jpg"
>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="hero__eyebrow">Variant A</p>
    <h1>Materialize everything. <em>Ship no JavaScript.</em></h1>
    <p>
      This is one of three parallel attempts at the Lossless Toolkit rebuild, each pushing a
      different hypothesis to its limit. This one takes the static-first position as far as it goes.
    </p>
    <dl class="statbar">
      <div><dt>Pages on disk</dt><dd>{pages}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>JS shipped</dt><dd>0</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Runtime deps</dt><dd>0</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Build deps</dt><dd>2</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__head"><h2>The thesis</h2></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A tag is a query. A vertical is a query. "Tools like this one" is a query. The usual move is
        to run those queries in the reader's browser — ship the whole index, ship a filter, and let
        the client compute. That is fast to build and it produces a site where nothing you are
        looking at has an address.
      </p>
      <p>
        The house vocabulary already names the alternative: <strong>content materialization</strong>.
        A query with a stable URL and a durable consumer should exist as a file. So every tag here is
        a page. Every vertical is a page. Every tool is a page. Adjacency is computed once, at build
        time, and written into the HTML.
      </p>
      <p>
        This matters more than usual for this particular site, because the primary way it gets used
        is someone sending one link to one person. A link is only worth sending if the thing it
        points at exists, has a title, and unfurls. Client-side state cannot be texted.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__head"><h2>What replaced the JavaScript</h2></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Tag filtering</strong> — <a href="/tools/">the tools index</a> has a multi-select
          facet filter built from <code>:has()</code> and checkbox state. Each facet contributes one
          CSS rule; checking several intersects them. Works with JS disabled.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Shareable filters</strong> — the CSS filter cannot be linked to, so every tag also
          gets a real route at <code>/tags/&lt;tag&gt;/</code> with its own title, description and
          share card. Ephemeral narrowing is CSS; linkable narrowing is a URL.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Theme modes</strong> — light, dark and vibrant, switched by radio inputs the root
          element reads with <code>:has()</code>. No Lossless site had ever shipped a mode; the dark
          palette existed and the other two are designed here.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Disclosure</strong> — <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>, which has been a native element
          for a decade.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Whole-card click targets</strong> — a stretched <code>::after</code> pseudo-element
          on the title link, not a click handler.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__head"><h2>What it cost</h2></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Honestly: the mode choice does not survive a navigation, because there is nowhere to persist
        it without script. Free-text search is absent — Pagefind is the house answer and Pagefind
        ships a WASM index. Analytics is absent for the same reason. Those are real, and they are
        the boundary this variant exists to find. The full accounting is in
        <code>VARIANT-NOTES.md</code> at the repo root.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
